A||N||12||joeformula88||Lowering an '88||||5||
Z||000000||joeformula88||03-20-2000||11:08 AM||||Couldn't find anyone (yet) on the forum who has lowered his '88 and shared it with the rest of us '88-ers. I'm very interested in the results, both looks and performance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Eric||03-20-2000||11:33 AM||||I don't know if I've ever seen a lowered 88, but I have seem some earlier years lowered and they look really good.  The problem with the 88s is you can't just cut the springs or install lowering springs because they throw off the suspension geometery and you wind up with a worse handling car than when you started.  The best way to do it is with drop spindles in front and coil overs in back.  You've probably noticed that the back of the car is much lower than the front, so you may get away with just the spindles.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||03-20-2000||01:23 PM||||I have to disagree with Eric. I have an 88 Formula with 1" lowering springs I bought from the Fiero Store one year ago. The look is great and nothing is mesed up with the suspension. Handling is excellent and it stick better to the road. I had a 98 Corvette to compare with and let me tell you that the difference was minimal. The Vette had wider tires to help. Something i can tell you is NOT to cut the original ones. I did it with the front ones (one turn) to start with and they feel pretty bad because they lost the travel. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||03-20-2000||05:31 PM||||I dont know where you got your info from Eric, but IMO it is wrong.  There are many 88's that have been lowered successfully with both spindles and springs.  The 88 suspension is slightly "more" adjustable than the previous years and thus makes for an even better platform for this.  The real problem lies in obtaining the springs or spindles.  The 88 rear uses the same strut setup as previous years (the mounting points are slightly different) so there are many options for lowering the tail end. <P>Only one company (Ryane Motorsports) offers front lowering spindles, but as with most of their products, the price is marked up way beyond what could be considered fair or reasonable.  <P>The Fiero Store used to sell lowering springs for the 88's, but no longer offers them.<P>That leaves ACE ( <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.americancustomeng.com/</A>  ) as the only remaining supplier of lowering springs for the 88's.  <P>I believe that RCC offers a reasonably priced coil over setup for the 88 front, but their web page seems to be missing at this time (not a good indication).<P>This trend towards discontinued or lack of support for the Fiero by both large aftermarket part suppliers and small operations is most concerning.  One should note that it is not just the 88 model year being effected by this, although it is much more apparent due to the "specific" parts it uses in some areas.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||03-20-2000||08:54 PM||||Oh boy.  Looks like my big mouth got me in trouble.  Let me first start by saying I don't put lowering springs specifically made for the 88 and cut stock springs into the same catagory.  Lowering springs usually are some kind of progressive rate jobbies that allow the suspension to drop a little more.  These are way better than the stock springs cut down a few inches, but not as good as drop spindles which allow the suspension to work the way it was intended (which I truely think is the best way).  My point is, you can probably use lowering springs with no noticable ill effects if you do indeed crave the look of a slammed Fiero.  In my personal experience, not having delt with lowered Fieros, other "riced up" cars tend to exhibit more body roll than their stock counterparts.  Maybe this is an illusion, but I think the lowered center of gravity counteracts some of the negative aspects and overall makes the car handle marginally better.<P>I wonder why the Fiero Store stopped selling the "lowering springs".  Was there something wrong with them or did the owners of 88s come to there senses and stop buying them?  In summation, stock suspension good, suspension modified with lowering spindles better, and lowering springs okay, but a lot better than cut stock springs.<P>LET THE FLAMES BEGIN!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula||03-20-2000||09:13 PM||||My 86 GT was lowered 4" in the back and 3" in the front, the Formula is only slightly higher then this with the oem replacement springs.<P>no need to lower an 88 IMO||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-20-2000||10:33 PM||||Mike is right, that RCC website vanished. It was there a week ago. Darrel told me over the phone that they needed to update that site, hopefully that's all they're doing. I just recently ordered rear coil-overs from RCC. They look great and I got them shipped immediately. Try this number: 817-536-3820, I also have an email address if someone really wants it. The Big Fiero Book lists those RCC 1988 front coil-overs (with Carrera shocks) as $495 a pair ($395 for 84-87).||||reg||1
Z||000007||mwbackus||03-21-2000||03:35 AM||||The front of evey "non lowered" Formula I have seen (including mine) is about half an inch higher than the rear.  It looks kind of dumb if ya ask me, but I do think the rear sets at about the right height.  I am not into "slamming" cars or "rice", and I do know enough about suspension dynamics to appreciate the difference in handling that a correctly lowered car exhibits.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||turbo86se||03-21-2000||11:55 AM||||My 86SE is lowered with ST springs, I love the way it looks and handles. Doesn't look slammed, and I have no problem going over speed bumps (just real slow!). There's still a gap between the fender and the tire, it just got rid of the "dune buggy look".<BR>My 88 coupe is not lowered. When I redo the suspension I will install the stock springs from a 88 formula. This will lower it slightly and "tighten" it up a bit, IMHO.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rodney||03-21-2000||02:12 PM||||I have a set of the Fiero Store 1988 front lowering springs for sale. Used in my 88 Mera for about 5000 miles. I switched to the drop spindles. Ride is now like it used to be. These ride a little rough but not terrible. Good thing about these slightly used ones is they have been used enough to get them to the height they will stay at for a long time. When new they do not lower the front end all the way. Takes some time and mileage to get them there. I went thru 2 alignments as they sagged. Buy new ones and you'll have the same thing. In time they will sag to a point where they stay for most of the rest of there life. These are about 1 3/16" drop. I had Ryane make me 1 1/4" drop spindles. When I lowered my 88 Mera these drop spindles were not available. I switched looking for a smoother ride to protect the fiberglass body. I am worried the rougher ride will in time not be good for it. The spindles are expensive. These drop springs are a reasonable way to drop the front of an 88. The rear is good as is. Just the front needs lowering. For sale at $120 and shipping. A good deal if you look at the hassle of getting new ones and having to break them in. They look as new and have a very tough black finish on them. I did not cut the bump stops when I had these in. It might have helped to cut a little off but I only occasionally bottomed them out over very bad pot holes or such.<P>Rodney Dickman<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000010||88 Fiero||03-21-2000||02:30 PM||||My 88 Formula (modified GT body) has a 1 1/2 inch cut springs and I did not experience any problem... I am running 17 inch wheels right now with a 255 in the rears and 225 in the fronts....||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||03-28-2000||10:16 AM||||This is lowered with Ace Eibach setup<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/done.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||joeformula88||03-28-2000||10:53 AM||||Phil,<BR>Can you tell us more about where you got your Eibach setup from. As far as I know Eibach doesn't make lowering springs/coilovers for '88 fiero's. How long ago did you get your hands on these and where. Please tell us more, because it sure looks good!! How does it drive???||||reg||5
